{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices in rising regimes (typical underprediction ~+0.5 to +2.5 €/t across much of the horizon). Apply an upward level shift when the last 5–10 days trend is positive.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: errors grow with horizon; the model tends to flatten/mean-revert too early. Increase the slope (trend component) for days 7–20 when recent momentum is positive; reduce premature convergence to a long-run mean.",
    "Spike handling: after upside breaks (e.g., jump into ~68–70), the model often underestimates the follow-through for ~1–2 weeks. Add a post-break continuation premium for ~5–10 days after a new 20–30d high.",
    "Late-horizon overshoot/whipsaw risk: in some runs the model projects large late declines (or large late rises) that do not materialize, indicating overreaction in the tail. Damp forecast changes beyond day ~20 (shrink deviations from the day-15 level by ~30–50%).",
    "Turning-point lag: when the market rolls over after a peak, the model can stay too high for a few days, but more commonly it misses the initial rebound after dips. Use asymmetric correction: faster upward adjustment after drawdowns than downward adjustment after peaks.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is often too smooth vs realized variability. Slightly widen day-to-day moves (especially days 1–10) toward recent realized volatility to avoid consistent underestimation of short-term swings."
  ]
}
